{"qid":"Q466803","names":["Late Pallava Empire"],"title":"Late Pallava Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Pallava dynasty ruled Tondaimandalam from their capital at Kanchipuram (modern Tamil Nadu), emerging as an independent power after the Satavahana decline around the third century CE. The dynasty reached its political and cultural apex under Mahendravarman I (r. c. 600–630) — who pioneered rock-cut temple architecture and composed Sanskrit plays — and Narasimhavarman I (r. 630–668), who sacked the Chalukya capital Vatapi in 642 and earned the title Mamalla, giving his name to the port of Mamallapuram. The Pallavas were in near-constant conflict with the Chalukyas of Badami to the north and the Chola and Pandya kingdoms to the south; the dynasty's last recorded king, Aparajitavarman, was defeated by the Chola ruler Aditya I around 897 CE, ending Pallava rule. Their architectural legacy — the Shore Temple, Descent of the Ganges relief, and rathas at Mamallapuram — shaped temple traditions across South and Southeast Asia.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Tondaimandalam core around Kanchipuram is well attested through copper-plate grants and inscriptions, but the dynasty's maximum territorial reach during Narasimhavarman I's reign is approximated from campaign records and is not precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Pallava dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pallava_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Pallava dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Pallava-dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Pallava-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Pallava dynasty","ruler